Costochondritis
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Sharp anterior chest wall pain, Reproducible tenderness at costochondral junctions, Absence of localized visible swelling, Normal ECG and cardiac troponins

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe left-sided chest pain radiating to the back, highly suspicious for an acute ischemic cardiac event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe, sharp anterior chest wall pain radiating to the left shoulder and back, causing profound distress and fear of myocardial infarction. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to perform a 12-lead ECG, assess hemodynamics, and definitively exclude life-threatening cardiac ischemia or pulmonary embolism prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have this sharp, stabbing pain right over my heart every time I take a breath, I think I'm having a heart attack."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 12-hour history of sharp, severe, and distinct localized pain over the anterior chest wall. The pain is exquisitely exacerbated by deep inspiration, coughing, or sudden upper body movements. The pain occasionally radiates to the left shoulder and back. The patient denies severe dyspnoea, palpitations, diaphoresis, or nausea. The patient reports a recent severe viral upper respiratory tract infection with a heavy cough resolving 3 days ago. The severe sharp pain is partially relieved by resting perfectly still.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently sitting rigidly still to avoid triggering the pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme anxiety regarding cardiac health; flatmate is prepared to call an ambulance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Anxious, sitting rigidly, taking shallow breaths to minimize pain.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals normal heart sounds, regular rate and rhythm, with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Chest wall examination reveals reproducible, severe, exquisite tenderness upon direct, focused palpation of the second to fifth costochondral junctions on the left side. Complete absence of localized, visible soft tissue swelling, erythema, or intense localized warmth over the joints.
Environmental Observations: Patient is slumped on a poorly supportive couch, adopting a kyphotic posture which compresses the anterior chest wall.
Medication Reconciliation: Took two paracetamol tablets with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Costochondritis - Status: Acute onset
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out clinically by the highly reproducible, positional, and pleuritic nature of the pain, absence of diaphoresis/nausea, and a normal 12-lead ECG performed on site.

Pulmonary Embolism - Unlikely given the highly localized point tenderness on the chest wall, normal SpO2, and absence of tachycardia or hemoptysis.

Tietze Syndrome - Ruled out due to the complete absence of visible or palpable localized soft tissue swelling over the costochondral junctions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to cardiac anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, acute left-sided chest pain radiating to the shoulder, a presentation that inherently carries a high risk of life-threatening acute coronary syndrome. An immediate, on-site clinical evaluation, including a 12-lead ECG and focused physical examination, was absolutely mandatory to assess for ischemic changes and point tenderness. By successfully reproducing the patient's exact pain via palpation of the costochondral junctions and confirming a normal ECG, the physician definitively diagnosed a benign musculoskeletal etiology. This immediate clinical exclusion of a myocardial infarction safely prevented an imminent, high-acuity ambulance transfer and emergency department presentation, strictly satisfying the requirements for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed normal 12-lead ECG on site. Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg stat for targeted anti-inflammatory effect.
Positive Behavioral Change: When coughing or sneezing, immediately cross your arms tightly across your chest to 'hug' a pillow. This physical splinting sharply reduces the mechanical excursion of the inflamed rib joints, rewarding the action with a significant reduction in sharp pain spikes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days if pain persists, or for serial ECG/Troponin if symptoms change in character.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the pain changes to a heavy, crushing central chest pressure, radiates down the left arm or into the jaw, or is associated with sweating, vomiting, or severe shortness of breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: An ECG is a snapshot; if the pain changes from sharp to heavy/crushing, it must be immediately reassessed in an emergency setting.
Clinical Course: Costochondritis is an inflammation of the cartilage connecting your ribs to your breastbone, typically triggered by the mechanical strain of a recent heavy cough. It is completely benign but can take several weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if the pain becomes a heavy weight on your chest, if you feel sick to your stomach, or if you break out in a cold sweat.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your heart and lungs are fine; the hinges where your ribs meet your breastbone are sprained and inflamed. Anti-inflammatories, rather than simple painkillers, are required to reduce this specific inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Apply a heating pad to the tender chest wall for 20 minutes, three times a day. The targeted heat increases local blood flow to the cartilage, speeding the clearance of inflammatory mediators and actively reducing the sharp, stabbing pain.

SMS INFORMATION

